---
// PHONE-ONLY: the explainer slides, the career prose and the appendix collapse to their headline.
//
// `data-phone-keep` — THE ONE THING A SECTION SAYS TO THIS FILE. The owner's phone shape is "a headline + an
// illustrative graph", not a headline alone: "probably a good iphone experience is very straight forward,
// heavily visual over texts". A section expresses that by marking ONE element `data-phone-keep`; the transform
// lifts exactly that element out of the collapsed body and parks it under the heading, so it is on screen while
// everything else is behind the tap. Nothing else about a section's markup changes, which is what lets each
// slide keep its desktop rendering byte-for-byte.
//
// A KEPT ELEMENT MAY ALSO NAME A DESTINATION — `data-phone-link` (+ `data-phone-cta` for the words), which wraps
// it in the card frame from components/PhoneExplainerCard.astro. This is the second half of the same finding
// that produced the collapse: measured against 17 hand-picked portfolios in real WebKit at 390x664, the phone
// homepage's two real outliers were type size and NOTHING TO TAP — 2 of 8 links on the first screen, both
// camouflaged inside a sentence, the other 6 at 93% page depth. The comparison set has exactly two working phone
// patterns and no third: an index, or chunked cards where every card is a link out. A collapsed section that
// shows one graphic linking nowhere is neither, so the graphic a section chose to keep is also the doorway it
// offers. Marking a keeper without a link is still valid — the appendix's cow is one, because it is a thing you
// PRESS, and wrapping that in a link that navigates is the one way to break it.
//
// WHY THIS IS A RUNTIME TRANSFORM AND NOT MARKUP. The disclosure could be authored in the .astro files, and
// that is the obvious way — but then three `button[aria-expanded]` controls exist on DESKTOP too, in its tab
// order and its accessibility tree, doing nothing. That is a real regression and an invisible one: no
// screenshot shows it. Building the disclosure in JS below the phone breakpoint means the HTML Astro emits is
// byte-for-byte what desktop ships today, so desktop is unchanged rather than merely looking unchanged.
//
// WHAT IT COSTS: a phone with JavaScript off gets today's long scroll. That is the current experience, not a
// new failure, but it does make this an enhancement layer rather than a guarantee.
//
// IT MOVES NODES, IT NEVER WRITES TEXT — and that is load-bearing for search, not just tidiness. Since
// mobile-first indexing, Google reads the PHONE rendering, and its guidance is explicit that content requiring
// a tap to load is not indexed ("Don't lazy-load primary content upon user interaction") while content merely
// collapsed into an accordion is fine and is the recommended way to save space. Rendering these bodies on tap
// would therefore have quietly cost the 1,606 words the three explainers carry. Everything here is
// appendChild of existing nodes: identity, listeners and the beat-steppers' element references all survive.
// The same rule governs the kept graphic below: it is LIFTED out of the collapsed body, not re-rendered.
---
<script>
  import { isPhone, PHONE_MAX_WIDTH } from '../lib/viewport';

  /**
   * The sections that collapse, in reading order. Nothing but ids: what survives inside each one is marked in
   * that section's own markup, so this file couples to no section's class names.
   *
   * #story earns its place by measurement rather than by symmetry: on an iPhone 14 it is 3.07 screens, made of
   * a 1.11-screen career list and 1.67 screens of "Why the climb" prose. Its h2 sits INSIDE .story-inner,
   * after the graph's slot, so the generic transform collapses exactly the prose and leaves the graph on screen.
   *
   * #appendix is the biggest win of the five: 2.71 screens, all of it three columns of titles, venues and
   * taglines that are one tap away on the pages they link to anyway. What may NOT go behind the tap is the
   * reference matter the owner named — the two headline numbers, the cow, and the footer. The footer is already
   * outside the collapsed region (it is nested AFTER the tan panel, so the walk below never reaches it); the
   * numbers and the cow are inside it and mark themselves.
   */
  const SECTIONS: readonly string[] = ['#choice', '#rules', '#solve', '#story', '#appendix'];

  type Undo = () => void;

  /** The card frame, defined once in components/PhoneExplainerCard.astro — see wrapAsCard(). */
  const CARD_CLASS = 'ph-card';

  /**
   * Wrap a kept element in the shared phone card frame, if it named a destination. Returns the frame, or null
   * when the element is not a doorway.
   *
   * ONE FRAME FOR THE WHOLE PHONE PAGE, and that is the point rather than a convenience: the survey behind this
   * work counted 37 instances of one card on colah's site and 56 on Pentagram's against this site's six
   * single-appearance compositions. `.ph-card` / `.ph-card-cta` / `.ph-card-arrow` are defined once, in
   * components/PhoneExplainerCard.astro's phone-only global block, which ships on the homepage because the three
   * explainer slides render it. THE COUPLING IS EXACTLY THOSE THREE CLASS NAMES — nothing here states a border,
   * a radius or a colour, and each section feeds the frame its palette through the inherited `--phc-*` custom
   * properties from its own stylesheet. If the frame is ever renamed, this constant moves with it and the
   * measurement that catches it is "the collapsed appendix's two numbers still sit in a bordered card".
   *
   * NO aria-label. The link's accessible name is its own content — the figures and then the call to action
   * ("~120% wealth compounded · OOS ’20–’25 … Read the research"), which is informative, cannot drift from what
   * is on screen, and satisfies label-in-name for free. The explainer cards need a label because a canvas has no
   * text; this one has nothing but text.
   */
  function wrapAsCard(el: HTMLElement): HTMLElement | null {
    const href = el.getAttribute('data-phone-link');
    if (!href) return null;
    const parent = el.parentElement;
    if (!parent) return null;

    const card = document.createElement('a');
    card.className = CARD_CLASS;
    card.href = href;
    parent.insertBefore(card, el);
    card.appendChild(el);

    // The words are the SECTION'S, read off its markup — the only text this file has ever written is none, and
    // that is worth keeping: a CTA typed here could contradict the link it sits on.
    const label = el.getAttribute('data-phone-cta');
    if (label) {
      const cta = document.createElement('span');
      cta.className = 'ph-card-cta';
      cta.textContent = label;
      // Decoration: the accessible name already says where this goes. Same treatment as `.wk-arrow`.
      const arrow = document.createElement('span');
      arrow.className = 'ph-card-arrow';
      arrow.setAttribute('aria-hidden', 'true');
      arrow.textContent = '→';
      cta.appendChild(arrow);
      card.appendChild(cta);
    }
    return card;
  }

  /** Put a framed element back where the frame stands and drop the frame (with the CTA it created). */
  function unwrapCard(card: HTMLElement): void {
    const parent = card.parentElement;
    if (!parent) return;
    for (const child of Array.from(card.childNodes)) {
      if (child instanceof HTMLElement && child.classList.contains('ph-card-cta')) continue;
      parent.insertBefore(child, card);
    }
    card.remove();
  }

  function collapseOne(section: HTMLElement, index: number): Undo | null {
    // The panel is the section's single full-bleed child; its own first child holds the column.
    const panel = section.firstElementChild as HTMLElement | null;
    const inner = panel?.firstElementChild as HTMLElement | null;
    // THE SECTION'S OWN HEADING, via the aria-labelledby it already declares — not `querySelector('h2[id]')`.
    // That shortcut returns the FIRST such heading in the subtree, which on #story is DescentPath's
    // "A descent, with one climb" nested inside the figure slot. The transform then collapsed from there and
    // swallowed the career list as well as the prose: measured 0.30 screens instead of 1.3, with the employer
    // names one tap away rather than on screen. aria-labelledby is unambiguous and is already the contract
    // these sections publish about which heading is theirs.
    const labelledBy = section.getAttribute('aria-labelledby');
    const heading = labelledBy ? document.getElementById(labelledBy) : null;
    if (!panel || !inner || !heading) return null;
    if (section.hasAttribute('data-phone-collapsible')) return null;   // already transformed

    const undos: Undo[] = [];

    // ── 1. The body: everything that follows the heading, AT EVERY LEVEL up to the column.
    // The three slides nest their heading at three different depths — Choice's h2 is a direct child of the
    // column, Rules' sits inside .ru-flow, Solve's inside .so-split > .so-say — so "the nodes after the
    // heading" is not one sibling list. Walking up and taking each level's following siblings is what makes
    // one implementation fit all three without hardcoding any of their structures.
    const panelIds: string[] = [];
    const boxes: HTMLElement[] = [];
    let level: HTMLElement = heading;
    let group = 0;
    while (level !== inner && level.parentElement) {
      const parent: HTMLElement = level.parentElement;
      const after: ChildNode[] = [];
      for (let n = level.nextSibling; n; n = n.nextSibling) after.push(n);
      if (after.length) {
        const box = document.createElement('div');
        box.setAttribute('data-phone-body', '');
        box.id = `phone-body-${index}-${group++}`;
        panelIds.push(box.id);
        boxes.push(box);
        parent.insertBefore(box, level.nextSibling);
        after.forEach((n) => box.appendChild(n));
        undos.push(() => {
          while (box.firstChild) parent.insertBefore(box.firstChild, box);
          box.remove();
        });
      }
      level = parent;
    }
    if (!panelIds.length) return null;

    // ── 2. THE KEPT ELEMENT — this is what makes the phone shape "kicker + headline + ONE graphic" instead of
    // "kicker + headline". A section marks one element `data-phone-keep`; the walk above has just swept it into
    // a body wrapper along with everything else, so here it is LIFTED back out and placed directly after the
    // heading, where it stays on screen while the section is closed.
    //
    // MOVED, NOT COPIED, and not re-created: `insertBefore` of the existing node keeps its identity, so the
    // slide's own script still steers the element that is on screen — clone it instead and every stepper,
    // observer and canvas context resolved at init would be driving the copy that got thrown away. Its
    // listeners survive, and — the search argument again — the words inside it are in the closed rendering
    // rather than behind a tap.
    //
    // MORE THAN ONE IS ALLOWED even though the slides use exactly one, because #appendix needs two disjoint
    // survivors (the numbers and the cow). They are inserted in document order after the heading, so "two
    // numbers, then the cow" reads down the page the way it does on desktop.
    const wanted = Array.from(section.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-phone-keep]'));
    // Only lift what the collapse actually swallowed — an element already outside the body (the appendix's
    // nested footer, say) is visible where it is, and moving it would reorder the page for no reason. And drop
    // anything nested inside another keeper, so a marked child cannot be torn out of a marked parent.
    const keepers = wanted.filter(
      (el) => boxes.some((b) => b.contains(el)) && !wanted.some((other) => other !== el && other.contains(el)),
    );
    let anchor: ChildNode = heading;
    for (const el of keepers) {
      const from = el.parentElement;
      const dest = anchor.parentNode;
      if (!from || !dest) continue;
      const back = el.nextSibling;                  // exact original position, for the teardown
      dest.insertBefore(el, anchor.nextSibling);
      el.setAttribute('data-phone-kept', '');
      // Runs BEFORE the body wrappers are unwrapped (undos are played in reverse), so `from` is still in the
      // document at its original place and `back` is still its child — the node lands exactly where it started.
      // Keepers restore in reverse too, which matters when one was the other's next sibling.
      undos.push(() => {
        el.removeAttribute('data-phone-kept');
        from.insertBefore(el, back);
      });
      const card = wrapAsCard(el);
      // THE NEXT KEEPER ANCHORS ON THE CARD, NOT ON THE ELEMENT INSIDE IT. `anchor.nextSibling` is how the
      // second keeper finds its place, and once the first one is wrapped its next sibling is the card's own
      // call-to-action row — so anchoring on `el` would deal the cow INTO the numbers' card, between the
      // figures and their link text.
      anchor = card ?? el;
      // Pushed AFTER the restore above so it plays BEFORE it: the frame is taken off while the element is still
      // inside it, and the keeper then travels home from the frame's parent. In the other order the restore
      // would pull the element out and leave an orphan <a> holding nothing but the CTA.
      if (card) undos.push(() => unwrapCard(card));
    }

    // ── 3. NO CONTROL. There is nothing to press, and that is the point.
    //
    // This was an accordion — a button inside each h2, ARIA pattern and all — and the owner removed it: "i guess
    // you dont even need that read the full explanation." They are right, and it is a simplification rather than
    // a compromise. A phone visitor was never going to tap through 1,600 words of explainer prose on a 390px
    // screen; the affordance was there to reassure ME that nothing had been taken away. What it actually added
    // was a control, a state, a chevron and an aria contract, all in service of a path nobody walks.
    // So the phone view is one-way: kicker, headline, one graphic. The prose stays in the DOM, hidden — which is
    // what keeps the round trip exact when the viewport leaves phone width, and what leaves the desktop markup
    // untouched.
    //
    // THE HONEST COST, recorded once: with no way to reveal it, that prose is not RENDERED on a phone, and since
    // mobile-first indexing reads the phone rendering, those words likely stop counting toward what the homepage
    // ranks for. Bounded — /research, /writing and /experience carry the substantive terms on their own pages,
    // and nobody searches for an explainer — and the owner chose the reading experience knowingly, twice.
    section.setAttribute('data-phone-collapsible', '');
    undos.push(() => section.removeAttribute('data-phone-collapsible'));

    return () => undos.reverse().forEach((u) => u());
  }

  let undoAll: Undo | null = null;

  function apply(): void {
    undoAll?.();
    undoAll = null;
    if (!isPhone()) return;
    const undos = SECTIONS.map((sel, i) => {
      const el = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>(sel);
      return el ? collapseOne(el, i) : null;
    }).filter((u): u is Undo => !!u);
    if (undos.length) undoAll = () => undos.forEach((u) => u());
  }

  document.addEventListener('astro:page-load', apply);

  // Re-decide when the breakpoint is crossed, and UNDO cleanly when leaving phone width — a rotated phone or a
  // resized window must not be left with a half-transformed DOM. Same reasoning as the deck's gate.
  if (typeof window !== 'undefined' && typeof window.matchMedia === 'function') {
    window.matchMedia(`(max-width: ${PHONE_MAX_WIDTH}px)`).addEventListener('change', apply);
  }
</script>

<style is:global>
  /* Global, not scoped: these elements are created at runtime, so Astro's scoped data attribute is not on
     them. Everything here is inside the phone breakpoint, so desktop cannot be reached by it. */
  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    /* One rule, and no second state. The prose is hidden and stays hidden: there is no toggle, so there is no
       [data-phone-open], no chevron, no focus ring and no aria contract to keep honest. The button, its ::after
       chevron, its rotation and its reduced-motion transition were all deleted with the control they served —
       dead CSS for an element that can no longer exist is a false clue about how the page works. */
    [data-phone-collapsible] [data-phone-body] { display: none; }

    /* WITHOUT THIS THE COLLAPSE ACHIEVES NOTHING. global.css gives every slide `min-height: 100vh` so that it
       "owns the screen" — a rule that exists FOR THE DECK. With the deck off on phones a collapsed explainer
       would still reserve a full screen and the page would not get shorter at all. The hero and the interlude
       keep theirs: the hero is a composition that wants the screen, and the tagline's whole point is landing
       alone in the sky.
       #APPENDIX IS DELIBERATELY NOT IN THIS LIST, by measurement rather than by principle: collapsed it is
       876px of an iPhone 14's 664 (541px of tan panel + a 335px footer), so 100vh is not what is setting its
       height and zeroing it would change nothing. The tallest phone tested, 430x932, lands at 932 against 933px
       of content — still not binding. It also holds the footer, and the footer is the one thing on the page
       that has earned reaching the bottom of the screen. If a future edit makes the appendix short enough for
       100vh to start padding it, add it here and re-measure `airBelowCow`. */
    .is-slides > section#choice,
    .is-slides > section#rules,
    .is-slides > section#solve,
    .is-slides > section#story { min-height: 0; }
  }
</style>
